<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/9th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Verdun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/843">843 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> was the agreement of 843 CE that divided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> among the three surviving grandsons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>: Lothar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>. It created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> and a middle kingdom stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Accounts of the Carolingian Empire treat it as a key stage in the empire's decline and a starting point for the later development of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun.jpg" alt="Treaty of Verdun" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_the_Pious_blessing_the_division_of_the_Carolingian_Empire_in_843_into_West_Francia,_Lotharingia,_and_East_Francia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
